3	 *
4	 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
5	 * THE INCIDENT THIS EXISTS FOR (2026-07-26)
6	 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
7	 * An agent ran `prisma/seed.ts` from a scratch working tree. That tree had its
8	 * `node_modules` SYMLINKED to /opt/nxeon/node_modules, so `@prisma/client`
9	 * resolved through the symlink, and Prisma's dotenv loader walked up from the
10	 * REAL module location and loaded `/opt/nxeon/.env` — the PRODUCTION env. The
11	 * seed therefore connected to the production database `nxeon` while every human
12	 * signal (the cwd, the checkout, the intent) said "scratch".
13	 *
14	 * It happened to be a pure `upsert` seed, so it did no damage. That is luck, not
15	 * a control. The same accident with `prisma db push --accept-data-loss`, or with
16	 * a seed that had grown a `deleteMany`, destroys a live hosting platform's
17	 * customer records.
18	 *
19	 * The root cause is NOT "the seed was careless". It is that a writing script
20	 * inherits its target from ambient environment resolution — a mechanism with no
21	 * confirmation step, which resolves silently and can be wrong for reasons that
22	 * are invisible at the call site (a symlink three directories away).
23	 *
24	 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
25	 * THE CONTROL
26	 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
27	 * A writing script must state, out loud, which database it believes it is about
28	 * to write to. The operator states it independently, via `NXEON_DB_TARGET`. The
29	 * script runs only if the two agree.
30	 *
31	 *     NXEON_DB_TARGET=nxeon_staging npx tsx prisma/seed.ts
32	 *
33	 * This is deliberately NOT a "--force"/"--yes" flag. A blanket confirmation
34	 * answers "do you want to proceed?", and in the incident above the honest answer
35	 * was yes — the operator did want to seed, just not THAT database. Naming the
36	 * target answers the question that was actually wrong. An operator who types
37	 * `NXEON_DB_TARGET=nxeon_scratch` while the environment silently resolves to
38	 * `nxeon` gets a refusal and a diff, which is exactly the moment the symlink
